state.counter += 1 is a read-modify-write sequence, not atomic. With concurrent requests, two threads can do:

Thread A reads counter = 41
Thread B reads counter = 41
Thread A writes 42
Thread B writes 42 (lost update)
So one increment disappears.

A likely failing input program is any burst of concurrent /run-simulation requests.

We simulate this in StressTest.scala


Efficient fix: use an atomic counter:


import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger

class ServerState {
  private val counter = new AtomicInteger(0)

  def nextRequestId(): Int = counter.incrementAndGet()
  def resetCounter(): Unit = counter.set(0)
  def currentCounter: Int = counter.get
}


Then in route logic:

Replace state.counter += 1; val cnt = state.counter with val cnt = state.nextRequestId()
Replace reset assignment with state.resetCounter()
AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet() guarantees no lost increments and gives each request a unique number under concurrency.


PS: in the implementation of the server resetCounter() was replaced by clear() since we also want to reset the log queue.